# ── Single-group Fleet Alerts Bot — deployment template ─────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env and fill it in for the ONE company and ONE Telegram group this
# deployment serves. Every event type, every vehicle, every provider (Motive and/or
# Samsara) goes to that one group — there is no per-vehicle group routing here, unlike
# the single-company branch this was forked from. To onboard another company, clone this
# branch and create a fresh .env + database.

# Telegram bot token from @BotFather
BOT_TOKEN=123456:your-token-here

# Bootstrap super-admin Telegram IDs (comma-separated) — the MAINTAINER's accounts.
# They can manage other admins, and they are HIDDEN from the in-bot 👥 Admins panel:
# the company's super admin sees only the team they added themselves.
# Hiding is display-only — these ids keep full access and receive every alert; they
# just can't be deactivated, removed, or handed the super role by anyone else.
# To make one visible again, remove it from this list; its access survives.
ADMINS=11111111,22222222

# PostgreSQL connection string
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:password@localhost:5432/fleet_alerts

# ── The company this deployment serves ──────────────────────────────────────────
COMPANY_SLUG=hf
COMPANY_NAME=HF Trucking

# The one Telegram group this deployment talks to — every event type, every vehicle,
# every provider. Telegram group ids are negative (e.g. -1001234567890).
#
# This value only SEEDS the group on first boot. From then on the database is
# authoritative, so the bot can self-heal when Telegram upgrades a basic group to a
# supergroup (which changes its chat id) without losing track of where alerts go.
# Changing this after the first boot has no effect — see utils/db_api/groups.py.
GROUP_CHAT_ID=-1001234567890

# Minimum speeding severity to alert on: low | medium | high | critical
SPEEDING_MIN_SEVERITY=high

# ── Admin Mini App ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ON RAILWAY, LEAVE THIS BLANK. Railway publishes the service's own public hostname to
# the container as RAILWAY_PUBLIC_DOMAIN, and the bot reads that — so the panel works
# after a deploy with nothing to paste. (If the service has no public domain yet, add one
# under service → Settings → Networking → Public Networking.)
#
# Set it only to override that: a custom domain, or a proxy in front of the service.
# Full base URL, no trailing slash, e.g. https://panel.yourcompany.com — and it must be
# https, because Telegram refuses to open a Mini App over http.
#
# Either way the panel is served at <URL>/panel/, and a "🖥 Admin Panel" button appears
# on the keyboard of anyone who passes the admin check. With no URL from either source
# the routes still answer, but nothing advertises them — the right state for a deployment
# that has no public domain.
#
# That keyboard button is used instead of the bot's ☰ menu button on purpose: a menu
# button is set once for every private chat, so every driver who DMs the bot would see
# "Admin Panel" and get a permission error for tapping it. The keyboard only goes to
# admins.
WEBAPP_URL=

# ── Samsara (leave blank if this company has no Samsara fleet) ───────────────────
SAMSARA_API_KEY=
SAMSARA_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ── Motive / KeepTruckin (leave blank to skip webhook signature verification) ────
MOTIVE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Read-only Motive API token. Used to confirm a crash detection against Motive's API
# ~3 min after the webhook, before alerting — Motive withdraws the detections its review
# rejects, and that is what separates a false crash from a real one. Leave blank and
# crashes still alert, but each one is tagged "Unconfirmed".
MOTIVE_API_KEY=
